00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:23.780
So, you know, there's that.

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:33.220
But also in terms of things that will hurt you in this game, it's really strange that the game seems to be littered with both up arrows and down arrows.

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:39.580
Now the up arrows bring your life back just a little bit, but the down arrows crush your life.

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:47.860
More than any enemy in this game really can, other than some bigger projectile fireballs and, and the, the mini bosses of each stage.

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:51.280
Otherwise, these down arrows are the biggest thing.

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:54.740
Like, I, I honestly think two or three are enough to kill you.

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:56.480
It's crazy to me.

00:19:56.740 --> 00:20:08.280
Why would you, on top of having enemies on the screen, enemies that can spawn from you breaking up terrain, why do you feel the need to also have, uh, power-ups that can hurt your character?

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:10.480
Isn't that what the enemies are for?

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:18.860
I feel like the feature of that hurts the game and my enjoyment, like my replayability, because now I have to dodge these down arrows.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:26.440
And yeah, it's kind of cute that they put an up arrow around a bunch of down arrows, so you have to kind of figure out your way to maneuver into it.

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:38.020
But they really should have either abandoned that concept altogether, or they should have made it so that the down arrows and the up arrows both just ever so slightly raise and decrease health.

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:43.940
Because there's something that pop up so frequently that you're not really ever going to find yourself without need of them.

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:55.940
Yeah, when I was reading about them in the manual, I, like, just, in the description, I thought, okay, these are just going to sometimes be around and not be the dominant feature of a freshly mowed lawn.

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:58.240
On our mode, uh, level.

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:04.040
Um, I, I thought it was going to be, like, any other don't pick this up, which is be, like, occasionally.

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:13.500
Yeah, I think we wouldn't even, we wouldn't even notice how stupid this is if they just simply took that down arrow sprite and replaced it with, like, a spike sprite.

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:15.920
Then it's like, okay, yeah, that's just, like, that makes sense.

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:22.580
This just almost feels like they just didn't, they just, I don't know, like, couldn't be bothered to just come up with, like, an interesting thing.

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:26.200
Yeah, it's not a pickup, it's just a, it's just a hazard in the environment.

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:31.000
Right. Yeah, I was waiting for the left and right arrows.

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:32.160
I wanted to see what they would do.

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:35.140
But they do, they do kind of show up, uh, in the final level.

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:36.320
Oh, that's, that's right.

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:55.200
These individual stages act as the training stages for each character, where you level up your character, and then it's based on whatever you collect within their particular level.

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:59.220
So, once you finish the knight stage, that's, like, it.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:02.860
Now you moved on to the wizard, then the monster, then the thief.

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:07.760
Assuming that you get through all four of those stages, you do go to the final stage.

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:17.980
But before we talk about that final stage, I guess I just want to talk about the resetting factor of going from knight to wizard to monster to thief.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:29.220
This is a game that all the levels are pretty short, given the, like, maybe they're a little longer than the typical shmup level, but considering there's only one for each of them, it's not terribly long.

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:33.340
However, when you first start, you're very weak.

00:22:33.340 --> 00:22:50.000
And, you know, it doesn't take too long to get powerful, but that's something that no matter what you did with the knight, now you are going back to basically being the knight again, only now disguised as a wizard when you go to his stage, you know?

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:55.860
It's, there's no actual, like, oh, because I did so well in the knight stage, now I have this.

00:22:55.960 --> 00:23:01.820
It's like, no, you'll just really have something at the end of the game, which I don't think pays off well, but what did you guys think about that?

00:23:01.820 --> 00:23:16.920
This could have been fixed, like, whether you were playing as, like, you know, the cook, the thief, his wife and her lover, whether it's one or the other, you would, this wouldn't be a problem if you were playing a different kind of character.

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:32.960
Like, yeah, if the knight was melee somehow, and if the wizard was the ranged one and the monster had maybe more of an area of effect sort of thing, and I don't know what the thief would do.

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:34.400
I don't think that makes sense at all.

00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:37.940
But if that was any different, that would be fine.

00:23:37.940 --> 00:23:43.740
